Here is the commit message:
bpf: Fix unsound scalar forking in maybe_fork_scalars() for
 BPF_OR

maybe_fork_scalars() is called for both BPF_AND and BPF_OR when the
source operand is a constant.  When dst has signed range [-1, 0], it
forks the verifier state: the pushed path gets dst = 0, the current
path gets dst = -1.

For BPF_AND this is correct: 0 & K == 0.
For BPF_OR this is wrong:    0 | K == K, not 0.

The pushed path therefore tracks dst as 0 when the runtime value is K,
producing an exploitable verifier/runtime divergence that allows
out-of-bounds map access.

Fix this by passing env->insn_idx (instead of env->insn_idx + 1) to
push_stack(), so the pushed path re-executes the ALU instruction with
dst = 0 and naturally computes the correct result for any opcode.

Fixes: bffacdb80b93 ("bpf: Recognize special arithmetic shift in the verifier")
Signed-off-by: Daniel Wade <danjwade95@gmail.com>
Reviewed-by: Amery Hung <ameryhung@gmail.com>
Acked-by: Eduard Zingerman <eddyz87@gmail.com>
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/20260314021521.128361-2-danjwade95@gmail.com
Signed-off-by: Alexei Starovoitov <ast@kernel.org>

Here is the root cause:
The `maybe_fork_scalars()` function, when handling `BPF_AND` and `BPF_OR` with the destination register having a signed range [-1,0], forks the verifier state by setting the pushed path's destination to 0 and skipping the current instruction (`env->insn_idx + 1`). This is correct for `BPF_AND` because `0 & K = 0`, but for `BPF_OR`, `0 | K = K`, not 0. The pushed path incorrectly tracks `dst = 0` instead of the actual result `K`, creating a divergence between the verifier's static analysis and the runtime execution. This unsoundness can be exploited to allow out-of-bounds map access. The fix re-executes the current instruction on the forked path (`env->insn_idx`), ensuring the correct result is computed for any operation.

Here is the patch:

 static int maybe_fork_scalars(struct bpf_verifier_env *env, struct bpf_insn *insn,
 			      struct bpf_reg_state *dst_reg)
 {
 	struct bpf_verifier_state *branch;
 	struct bpf_reg_state *regs;
 	bool alu32;

 	if (dst_reg->smin_value == -1 && dst_reg->smax_value == 0)
 		alu32 = false;
 	else if (dst_reg->s32_min_value == -1 && dst_reg->s32_max_value == 0)
 		alu32 = true;
 	else
 		return 0;

-	branch = push_stack(env, env->insn_idx + 1, env->insn_idx, false);
+	branch = push_stack(env, env->insn_idx, env->insn_idx, false);
 	if (IS_ERR(branch))
 		return PTR_ERR(branch);

 	regs = branch->frame[branch->curframe]->regs;
 	if (alu32) {
 		__mark_reg32_known(&regs[insn->dst_reg], 0);
 		__mark_reg32_known(dst_reg, -1ull);
 	} else {
 		__mark_reg_known(&regs[insn->dst_reg], 0);
 		__mark_reg_known(dst_reg, -1ull);
 	}
 	return 0;
 }



